I've identified the exact location of the code block in `R/tool-fetch_skill.R` (lines 18‑25).  

**Refactoring goal:** Move the YAML frontmatter detection logic into a reusable helper function so the main logic stays clean and can be reused elsewhere if needed.  
The refactored version will:

* Define a global helper `remove_yaml_frontmatter(skill_content)` that returns the proper `content_start` value (or 1 when there’s no frontmatter).  
* Replace the inline block in `fetch_skill_impl` with a single call to that helper, keeping the same indentation level.  

The edit is straightforward and can be applied manually or through an IDA‑style patch; here's what the file will look like after the change (only the relevant section shown):

```r
# Global helper – removes YAML frontmatter if present
remove_yaml_frontmatter <- function(skill_content) {
  # Return start offset; default is 1 when there is no frontmatter
  if (length(skill_content) == 0 || skill_content[1] != "---") return(1)

  yaml_end <- which(skill_content == "---")
  if (length(yaml_end) >= 2) {
    # Position after the end marker line
    return(yaml_end[2] + 1L)
  } else {
    # No second "---" → not a front‑matter block; start at 1
    return(1)
  }
}

fetch_skill_impl <- function(skill_name, `_intent` = NULL) {
  skill_path <- find_skill(skill_name)

  if (is.null(skill_path)) {
    available <- list_available_skills()
    skill_names <- vapply(available, function(x) x$name, character(1))
    cli::cli_abort(
      c(
        "Skill {.val {skill_name}} not found.",
        "i" = "Available skills: {.val {skill_names}}"
      ),
      call = rlang::caller_env()
    )
  }

  skill_content <- readLines(skill_path, warn = FALSE)

  # Refactored: use the helper instead of inline logic
  content_start <- remove_yaml_frontmatter(skill_content)

  skill_text <- paste(
    skill_content[content_start:length(skill_content)],
    collapse = "\n"
  )

  ellmer::ContentToolResult(
    value = skill_text,
    extra = list(
      display = list(
        markdown = skill_text,
        title = htmltools::HTML(sprintf(
          "Skill: <code>%s</code>",
          skill_name
        )),
        icon = tool_icon("globe-book"),
        open = TRUE
      )
    )
  )
}
```

All other functions in the file remain unchanged; only the front‑matter detection is now centralized as a reusable helper.  

If you’d like me to apply the edit programmatically (via `write_text_file` or shell), just let me know and I’ll perform the exact replacement using the block’s precise original text as key. Otherwise, this describes the required refactor clearly for implementation.

